---
id: org.dxos.review.agent-corrections
name: AgentCorrectionRules
version: 0.1.0
---

Rules mined from the corrections maintainers made to coding agents' work in
~3,400 Claude Code sessions across `dxos/dxos` and `dxos/edge` (export of
2026-09-27). Only corrections to the code or design an agent produced count —
rejected approaches, introduced bugs, "we don't do X here" — not workflow
complaints. Each rule merges every correction of one kind; the `Source:` line
gives how many independent corrections back it and quotes one. Rules already
covered by the other files in this directory were not repeated here. Each
`rule` block below is discovered by the agentic-review harness, matched against
the files its globs select, and applied by a focused subagent.

## Extensions

| Term   | URI                   |
| ------ | --------------------- |
| `rule` | org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0 |

## Effect

```mdl
rule no-try-catch-in-effect-code: Handle errors with Effect's error channel, never try/catch
  The codebase writes failure handling with Effect, so a `try { } catch` in
  new code bypasses typed errors and usually swallows a failure the caller
  should see. Flag a new `try`/`catch` block in TypeScript source, including
  one wrapping a caller-supplied predicate "to be safe", and hand-rolled
  `JSON.parse` plus property checks for a wire message in Effect code. Do not
  flag a platform boundary that is immediately wrapped (`Effect.try`,
  `Effect.tryPromise`) into a typed error, a sync browser-callback contract
  that cannot be Effect, or test code asserting a throw. Fix: model the
  failure as a typed error (`Effect.try`, `Effect.catchTag`), decode messages
  with `Schema.decodeUnknown`, and let a caller's bug surface instead of
  swallowing it.
  Source: 5 corrections, e.g. "never use try-catch. We write stuff with effect and use effect native error handling."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: \btry\s*\{|JSON\.parse
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule call-effect-api-not-promise-bridge: Inside Effect code, call the Effect form of an API, never its Promise twin
  Bridging out to a Promise variant and back (`Effect.promise(() =>
  x.invokePromise(...))`, `Effect.promise(() => runtime.runPromise(...))`)
  drops typed errors and output, loses the span, and usually needs an extra
  capability lookup the Effect form does not. Flag `Effect.promise` /
  `Effect.tryPromise` wrapping a `*Promise` method of an API that also
  exports an Effect form (e.g. `Operation.invoke`), or a service converted
  to `async` where its siblings are Effect. Do not flag a true platform
  boundary (dynamic `import()`, browser callback APIs) with no Effect form.
  Fix: `yield*` the Effect form directly.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "It's really silly to use effect promise here and invoke promise when you can just use invoke directly."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: Effect\.(try)?[pP]romise\(|runPromise|invokePromise
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule retries-via-schedule-with-evidence: Retry with Effect.retry and a Schedule, and only when the failure is shown to be transient
  Hand-written retry loops are long and easy to get wrong, and a retry added
  because an error "looks transient" hides a permanent bug. Flag a counter /
  `for (let attempt ...)` / sleep-and-backoff loop in Effect-capable code,
  and a new retry whose PR shows no evidence that a second attempt succeeds.
  Do not flag retries already expressed with `Schedule`, or network-edge
  retries backed by data. Fix: `Effect.retry(Schedule.exponential(...)
  .pipe(Schedule.upTo(...)))`, or delete the retry if the condition is
  permanent.
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "we should validate if the retries are necessary at all, that could maybe be removed entirely."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: attempt|retries|retry
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule log-fire-and-forget-rejections: A fire-and-forget promise logs its rejection, never swallows it
  `.catch(() => {})` on a `void`-ed call makes a startup or background failure
  invisible. Flag `.catch(() => {})`, `.catch(() => undefined)`, or an empty
  `catch {}` on a fire-and-forget call. Do not flag a catch that handles one
  named expected error and says why. Fix: `.catch((err) => log.catch(err))`
  or the Effect equivalent.
  Source: 1 correction showing the exact replacement: "void client.initialize().catch((err) => log.catch(err))".
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: \.catch\(\s*\(\)\s*=>\s*(\{\s*\}|undefined|null)\s*\)
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

## ECHO

```mdl
rule mutate-echo-arrays-with-splice: Remove or reorder entries of an ECHO array with splice, never by reassigning the array
  Assigning a rebuilt array (`obj.items = obj.items.filter(...)`,
  `obj.items = arrayMove(...)`) replaces the whole list, so reactivity fires
  for every element and concurrent CRDT edits to the list are lost. This
  extends `obj-update-push` (appends) to removals and reorders. Flag a
  wholesale reassignment of an existing ECHO array field inside `Obj.update`
  or on a live object. Do not flag initial construction (`Obj.make`) or a
  deliberate full replacement from an external source. Fix: `splice` at the
  affected index.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "this is a reactivity anti-pattern ... splicing the array rather than reassigning the whole thing."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: =\s*\[\.\.\.|\.filter\(|arrayMove
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule ref-collections-via-query: List, count, or walk referenced objects through a query or Database.load, not by reading the ref array
  A ref array still holds refs to deleted or unloaded objects, so its
  `.length` is not a member count, and a synchronous `ref.target` walk sees
  only what happens to be loaded and returns an incomplete subtree. Flag
  `refs.length` shown or used as a count; iteration that resolves each ref and
  filters `isDeleted` by hand; sweeping dangling refs out of arrays after a
  delete; a recursive helper over `ref.target` in plain sync code. Do not flag
  code that needs the stored order (render the array, count with a query),
  identity-only comparisons of DXNs, or serialization/migration of raw refs.
  Fix: `Query.select(Filter.entity(x)).reference(...)` / `.referencedBy(...)`
  / `Filter.childOf`, which exclude deleted objects, or make the walk an
  Effect that `Database.load`s each target.
  Source: 7 corrections, e.g. "If you want a count that takes deletions into account, it should be based off of a query rather than just counting the elements of the array."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: \.target\b|\.length
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule operations-resolve-database-from-service: An operation never takes a database as input; it yields Database.Service
  Passing `db` through operation input couples the schema to a live handle
  and makes the operation uncallable by an agent or over RPC. Flag an
  operation input schema with a `db`/`Database` field, callers passing
  `{ db }` in operation input, or a handler reaching
  `client.spaces.get(spaceId).db`. Do not flag a `spaceId` or Ref input that
  identifies a target. Fix: drop `db`, pass `{ spaceId }` in the invoke
  options, and `yield* Database.Service` in the handler.
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "Operation should not take a database as an input, it should just pass the space ID in the invoke options and use the database service in the handler."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: db:|\.spaces\.get\(
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule typename-via-schema-not-string: Refer to a type's typename through its schema, never a string literal
  A literal `'org.dxos.type.document'` in a predicate, matcher, or key
  silently stops matching when the type is renamed or versioned. Flag a
  typename-shaped string literal outside the schema's own declaration where
  the schema is importable. Do not flag the declaration itself, tests that
  assert the serialized value, or a site where importing the schema would
  break dependency direction. Fix: `Type.getTypename(Schema)` or
  `Obj.instanceOf(Schema, obj)`.
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "don't hardcode typename strings in tour predicates."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: ['"]org\.dxos\.(app\.)?type\.
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule persisted-shape-changes-adapt-at-consumer: Don't change a persisted serialization to fix one consumer; adapt at that consumer
  Changing how ECHO types serialize (JSON Schema emission, ref collapsing,
  stored type shapes) breaks every older reader of data already persisted.
  Flag edits to `toJsonSchema` / JSON Schema emission or another
  stored-and-replicated shape whose motivation is one consumer's problem (an
  MCP client, a projection), with no compatibility analysis of old readers.
  Do not flag read-path tolerance that accepts both shapes, or a change
  behind an explicit version/migration. Fix: rewrite the shape in the
  consumer layer that persists nothing, and leave a TODO for the upstream
  change.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "Is that a breaking change? ... that changes the JSON schema serialization of echo types that were persisting."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/core/echo/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: [Jj]son[Ss]chema
  unit: file
  context: diff
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule device-local-state-not-in-space: Per-device state lives in local persistence, not the replicated space
  Writing device-specific overrides into ECHO replicates them to every
  device and forces a second copy of the local values. Flag device-keyed
  preferences or overrides stored on space objects, or a local value
  duplicated into a synced store. Do not flag data other devices must see
  (presence, a device list). Fix: keep it in the local settings store or a
  localStorage-backed atom; the space holds only synced values.
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "Why do we store device overrides in echo at all? ... Just get stored in local storage."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/sdk/app-toolkit/src/**/*.ts
  grep: deviceKey|[Dd]evice
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule reference-definitions-by-object: An API that names a defined entity takes its definition object, not a string key or a new ref type
  A bare string key, or a new `XRef = { key: string }` wrapper, for a skill,
  operation, or other definition loses type checking and drifts from the
  definition's real key. Flag a new `*Ref` helper type or string-key field in
  an annotation or option that points at something already exported as a
  definition object with `.key`. Do not flag wire or serialized forms where
  only the string can travel. Fix: accept the definition (e.g.
  `Skill.Definition`) and read `.key` at the use site.
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "I don't know why we're creating this new type ... should take a skill definition, and then you can do the dot key."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: Ref\b|key:\s*string
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

## Atoms and reactivity

```mdl
rule atom-lifetime-bound-to-owner: An atom lives exactly as long as its owner; never pin it with keepAlive or a side registry
  Pinned atoms (`Atom.keepAlive`, `retainAtoms`-style flags, a mount-only
  hook) never expire and leak; an atom held by a class but evictable while the
  class is alive forces a duplicate `#fooValue` backing field to survive
  eviction; a private `Registry.make()` splits state from the app registry.
  Flag new `keepAlive`/pin options, a class keeping both a value field and an
  atom for the same data, and `Registry.make()` outside tests and stories.
  Do not flag removing an existing keepAlive, the owned-atom helper
  (`AtomEx.owned`), or idle-TTL configuration. Fix: use an owned atom tied to
  the owning class/closure, or let the registry's idle TTL expire it.
  Source: 6 corrections, e.g. "ensure that we aren't using keepalive, we should be using owned or expiring atoms instead."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: keepAlive|retain|Registry\.make\(|Atom\.(make|writable)
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-eager-read-before-subscribe: Don't read an atom just before subscribing to force it to build
  A `registry.get(atom)` whose value is discarded, placed before
  `registry.subscribe(atom, ...)` "because a subscription alone does not build
  the atom", papers over an ownership bug and spreads to every call site.
  Flag a discarded read immediately before a subscribe, or comments like
  "read first". Do not flag a read whose value is used. Fix: subscribe with
  `{ immediate: true }`, or fix the atom's ownership so its owner mounts it.
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "I don't want any of the eager reads, that's an anti-pattern."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: \.subscribe\(
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule atom-family-keys: Key atom families by one structural tuple and by identity, never a family of families
  A family returning a family leaks inner families nobody can dispose, and
  structurally hashing a live object (space, ECHO proxy, client) walks its
  getters, which can throw or run deep. Flag `Atom.family` whose value is
  another `Atom.family`, a family keyed by a live object with no
  `[Hash.symbol]`/`[Equal.symbol]`, and a module-level `WeakMap<object, Atom>`
  added to work around that. Do not flag keys that are primitives, DXNs, or
  already implement Hash/Equal (e.g. `Ref`). Fix: one family keyed by
  `Data.tuple(a, b)`; give the keyed type id-based Hash/Equal traits as `Ref`
  does.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "We should never have families of families. We should use the effect Tuple pattern as the key."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: Atom\.family
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-module-level-object-caches: Scope a WeakMap cache or FinalizationRegistry to its owner, never the module
  A module-level `new WeakMap()` / `new FinalizationRegistry()` tracking
  per-instance resources outlives every owner and cannot be disposed with
  one, and several such registries for one concept scatter its lifetime
  across files. Flag a top-level `const x = new (WeakMap|Map|FinalizationRegistry)`
  used to cache or track per-object state. Do not flag an immutable lookup
  table, or a documented singleton service. Fix: hold it on the owning class
  or in the closure that owns the resources, or carry the data on the value.
  Source: 5 corrections, e.g. "I prefer to avoid module level weak maps as much as possible."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: (^|\n)(export )?const \w+\s*(:[^=\n]+)?=\s*new (WeakMap|Map|FinalizationRegistry)\b
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule late-capability-read-via-atom: Read a capability that arrives later through its atom, not by gating activation on another plugin's event
  Adding `activatesOn: OtherPluginEvents.X` (or re-declaring that plugin's
  event constant, or firing it from a component) only to avoid a "client not
  ready" throw couples plugins through lifecycle events and duplicates their
  constants. Flag such an `activatesOn`, a copied event constant documented
  "must equal X", and a per-file re-implementation of an "initialized client"
  atom. Do not flag events the plugin really owns. Fix: `get(clientAtom)` (or
  the owning plugin's shared readiness atom) so the consumer re-evaluates
  when the capability arrives.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "Is getting it from the atom not better? It should then reactively update."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/plugins/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: activatesOn|Initialized
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

## Architecture and API surface

```mdl
rule vendor-code-in-vendor-extension: Vendor-specific mapping lives in the vendor's extension, not a generic package
  PostHog event names, vendor schema mapping, or a vendor SDK call inside a
  generic package (`@dxos/observability`, `@dxos/ai`, CLI core) pins every
  consumer to that vendor. Flag vendor names, event shapes, or SDK calls added
  to a generic package, and domain commands hard-wired into a host instead of
  contributed by the owning plugin. Do not flag the vendor's own extension
  module, or a vendor-neutral interface in the generic package. Fix: define
  the seam generically and implement the vendor half as an extension.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "the post hog part belongs in the post hog extension ... we should only have generic stuff in here."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: [Pp]ost[Hh]og|\$ai_|\$mcp_|signoz
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule always-on-behavior-built-in-not-a-seam: Build behavior every caller gets into the runtime, not a new capability or middleware
  A new capability, middleware contribution, or service layer whose only job
  is to add something every run must have (tracing, stamping spaceId on
  spans, attaching a tracer) adds a seam with one implementation and a way
  to forget it. Flag such a pluggable seam, or a new capability next to an
  existing one that could host it. Do not flag optional behavior with
  several real implementations. Fix: do it at the point of execution (the
  process manager adds the span attributes; the tracer is an
  application-level layer in every runtime), or fold it into the existing
  capability.
  Source: 4 corrections, e.g. "We don't need this special pluggable middleware to do this."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  unit: file
  context: diff, siblings
  system-one: off
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule generic-layer-takes-policy-as-parameter: A generic layer never names an app concept; the consumer passes it in
  `dependency-direction` catches the import; this catches the concept arriving
  without one. A generic engine (`@dxos/graph`, `@dxos/app-graph`, a builder)
  that hard-codes a constant, branch, or default for a concept a higher layer
  owns (the `companion` relation, deck retention, a workspace notion) works
  for one consumer and leaks that consumer's model downward. Flag such a
  special case in a generic package, and a policy interface at the generic
  level that duplicates state the consumer already owns. Do not flag
  primitives the generic layer itself defines. Fix: the generic layer accepts
  the concept as a parameter or an interface the consumer fulfils with its
  own state; the consumer performs the special action.
  Source: 5 corrections, e.g. "the companion relation definitely is an app-toolkit level thing, not app-graph."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/common/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/sdk/app-graph/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/sdk/app-framework/src/**/*.ts
  unit: file
  context: diff, package
  system-one: off
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-new-knob-for-derivable-value: Derive a value from an existing signal; don't add a flag, env var, or field for it
  Every new setting, persisted bit, env var, or declaration field is state
  that can disagree with the signal it duplicates, and a field nearly every
  registration repeats is a missing default. Flag a new boolean setting,
  persisted `*Dismissed`/`*Allowed` bit, env var (`DX_*_HOST`,
  `*_ENABLED`), or binding/extension field whose value is implied by an
  existing one (an existing env var, space encryption, presence of an
  identity, another field, a structural guarantee). Do not flag a genuinely
  new degree of freedom the PR names as such. Fix: read the existing signal,
  default the common case, derive the rest internally, and delete the knob.
  Source: 8 corrections, e.g. "there's likely a sensible default which covers most cases so we don't need to list it in all extensions."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: diff, siblings
  system-one: off
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule change-behavior-inside-existing-api: Put a fix or variant inside the existing API; don't grow a sibling, hybrid, or /lazy twin
  An optimization that adds public surface (`peekOrLoad`, `queryActivity`),
  a sibling method differing by one tweak (`loadIncludingDeleted`,
  `fooWithMapper`), or a parallel variant subpath (`mermaid/lazy`) makes
  callers choose between near-duplicates that drift apart. Flag such an
  addition when the existing entry point could take a fast path internally
  or an options argument. Do not flag two orthogonal primitives that compose
  cleanly (`peek` and `load`), or a variant with a distinct return type. Fix:
  do it under the hood (e.g. `load` returns synchronously for a loaded ref),
  add an option (`load(ref, { deleted: 'include' })`), or make the new
  implementation the default and delete the old.
  Source: 7 corrections, e.g. "why did the query api need to change for this change? can we not make this change under the hood only?"
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  unit: file
  context: diff, public-api
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule shared-api-default-unchanged: Changing a widely used API's behavior is an opt-in option; the default stays
  Flipping the default semantics of a shared annotation or helper (e.g.
  `SetParent` becoming first-wins) to serve one new call site changes every
  existing caller silently. Flag a diff that changes the default behavior of a
  widely imported API for a few new sites, or widens a boolean annotation
  with an ad hoc flag. Do not flag fixing a default that was wrong for every
  caller. Fix: add a structured option (`{ value = true, override }`) whose
  defaults reproduce today's behavior and opt in at the new sites.
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "we should preserve the default behaviour and add an option that we can use for collections and projects."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/core/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/sdk/**/src/**/*.ts
  unit: file
  context: diff, importers
  system-one: off
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-unused-new-api: Don't add or export API that nothing calls
  `delete-dead-code-after-migration` covers what a change leaves behind; this
  covers what it adds speculatively. Flag a new exported function, constant,
  getter, or option with no non-test caller in the PR or repo, or one
  exported only so a test can import it. Do not flag an extension point the
  PR names as intentionally public, or exports consumed elsewhere in the same
  PR. Fix: remove it or keep it module-private; test through the public API.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "there's a public api without any usage, did you remove that?"
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: (^|\n)export
  unit: file
  context: diff, importers
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule one-name-one-export-per-concept: One name and one export path per concept
  Two terms for one thing (sample space / space template, unfiled / hidden),
  an alias function that only calls another under a synonym
  (`isTelemetryDisabled` → `isObservabilityDisabled`), or one symbol
  re-exported from several namespaces (`GraphBuilder.Retention`,
  `AppGraphBuilder.Retention`) leaves readers guessing which is canonical.
  Flag each of these in the diff. Do not flag a `@deprecated` alias during a
  documented migration, or two genuinely distinct concepts named distinctly.
  Fix: pick the established term, keep the symbol in its owning module,
  delete aliases and re-exports, and update every usage.
  Source: 7 corrections, e.g. "why is region and retention exported in multiple namespaces, confusing, drop this and update usages."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  unit: file
  context: diff, siblings
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule symbol-lives-in-module-of-its-concern: Put a symbol in the module named for its concern, and name modules for what they hold
  `expandPath` in the `NotFound` namespace, a generic parent helper on
  `CollectionModel`, tour types in `AppCapabilities`, a membership helper in
  `operations/`, `binding.ts` beside `bindings.ts`, or a namespace coined
  from descriptive prose (`Canonical.follow`) all send readers to the wrong
  place. Flag an export added to a namespace or module unrelated to its
  purpose, a non-handler module under `operations/`, near-duplicate sibling
  file names, and a class named with an adjective or participle. Do not flag
  pre-existing placement the diff does not touch. Fix: move it to the module
  of its concern (the type's namespace, a feature namespace such as `Tour`),
  name files for their contents, and give classes noun names.
  Source: 8 corrections, e.g. "why is expandPath in the NotFound namespace? ... it makes no sense, can we find a better place for it?"
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: diff, siblings
  system-one: off
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule read-state-through-owning-api: Read shared config and consent through its owning API, not its backing file or key
  Reading `observability.yml` or a storage key directly scatters knowledge
  of where the value lives, so moving it later means finding every reader.
  Flag a direct `fs`/`localStorage`/KV read of a value a module already
  exposes through an accessor. Do not flag the accessor's own
  implementation. Fix: call the accessor.
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "the value of calling the API is a centralized path so that if we change where that data is coming from later we don't have to find all of the random file accesses."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: readFileSync|readFile\(|localStorage\.getItem
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-silent-storage-fallback: Never fall back silently to memory storage when persistent storage fails
  Running on an in-memory backend after OPFS/SQLite/IndexedDB fails to open
  hides the user's real data and discards everything they create. Flag a
  catch or probe branch that swaps a persistent backend for `layerMemory`, an
  in-memory store, or a no-op store; a log-and-continue on that path; a
  retry around a storage probe with no evidence the failure is transient. Do
  not flag explicit memory storage chosen by configuration (tests, a guest
  profile). Fix: fail startup with an error naming the original cause (the
  underlying DOMException, not its wrapper).
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "It should never fall back to memory."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: layerMemory|[Mm]emory[A-Z]?\w*(Storage|Store|Backend)|inMemory
  unit: file
  severity: error
```

```mdl
rule no-consumer-toggled-global-mode: Don't fix a timing problem by letting one consumer flip a temporary mode on a shared engine
  A flag like `_expedited` that one UI handler sets and a `setTimeout`
  clears, or a `flushSync` inserted to force ordering, hard-codes one
  consumer's needs into a shared scheduler and races every other consumer.
  Flag such a call-site mode toggle on a shared graph, store, or scheduler.
  Do not flag scoped batching APIs whose effect ends with the callback
  (`Atom.batch`). Fix: change the engine's policy generically (e.g. apply
  updates to already-materialized nodes immediately within a frame budget).
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "this expedite concept, I find confusing and sketchy ... a very leaky abstraction."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: flushSync|expedit
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule workaround-is-documented-and-removable: A workaround names its proper fix, its owner, and when it can be deleted
  A stopgap with no trail outlives its reason: nobody knows whether the
  upstream bug shipped a fix, or what the right solution was. Flag a new
  shim, browser/UA gate, lock, duplicated config, boundary rewrite, or
  `patches/` entry that works around third-party or upstream behavior without
  a `TODO(owner)` naming the proper fix and why it is deferred, a link to the
  upstream issue where one exists, and a removal condition; flag a workaround
  the PR's own evidence shows is no longer needed. Do not flag permanent
  design decisions. Fix: keep the minimal fix, gate it behind one switch,
  add the TODO and link next to it, and delete it once measured unnecessary.
  Never patch a core dependency such as `effect` without explicit approval.
  Source: 8 corrections, e.g. "this is okay as long as there is a to-do noting that this should be generalized and it's just a work around."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
    - tools/**/*.ts
    - patches/**
  grep: [Ww]orkaround|shim|[Ww]eb[Kk]it|[Ss]afari|userAgent|HACK
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule measure-before-optimizing: A performance device needs an observed problem and a measurement
  `useDeferredValue`, `memo`, component splits, caches, memoize wrappers, or
  a dependency patch added "preventatively" add complexity that may do
  nothing (a deferred value with no `memo` below it skips no work). Flag a
  performance change justified only by reasoning ("should help with large
  docs"), with no profile, benchmark, or reproduction in the PR. Do not flag
  an optimization with before/after numbers beyond noise. Fix: remove it, and
  fix the cause (e.g. the stall) rather than guard the symptom.
  Source: 5 corrections, e.g. "did you observe an issue that required this or it was preventative?"
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: useDeferredValue|\bmemo\(|memoize|useMemo|cache
  unit: file
  context: diff, pr
  system-one: off
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule isolate-legacy-protocol-support: Keep support for an old protocol version in self-contained, removable modules
  Branches for a legacy protocol version (e.g. an older MCP spec) woven
  through the core request path make dropping it a hunt. Flag legacy-version
  branches interleaved in core logic, or compat code without a TODO naming
  when it goes. Do not flag a single version-negotiation switch that
  dispatches to legacy modules. Fix: prefer the new protocol in the core
  path; put legacy adapters in separate modules, each tagged
  `TODO(owner): remove once we drop <version>`.
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "support the old protocol in an easy to remove way with a to-do to remove it."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: [Ll]egacy|protocolVersion|\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule telemetry-raw-and-opt-in: Telemetry sends raw measurements, keys come from build secrets, and dev builds stay silent
  A committed `phc_...` key leaks into forks, a default that makes every
  local run or test report remotely floods the project with noise, and a
  hand-maintained per-model price table goes stale. Flag a literal analytics
  key in source, a default endpoint/key that turns on remote export for
  source or dev builds, and cost computed from a local pricing table. Do not
  flag published builds shipping production config, opt-in env vars, or
  costs reported by the provider. Fix: inject keys at build time from
  secrets, export from local builds only when explicitly configured, and
  record token counts and model id instead of cost.
  Source: 4 corrections, e.g. "We don't want all testing to send remote telemetry because that would create a whole bunch of noisy data."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/*.config.ts
    - scripts/**/*.mjs
  grep: phc_|POSTHOG|OTEL|[Pp]ricing|costPer|perMillion
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

## Plugins

```mdl
rule workerd-variant-imports-conditioned-barrel: A plugin's workerd build is the `dx.config.ts` modules scoped by `environments` plus a workerd-conditioned `#operations`, never a hand-picked capability module
  Reaching past the config for one capability or handler module to dodge
  React works for one plugin and leaves the rest leaking UI into the worker.
  Flag a `dx.config.ts` module that is headless-capable but lists no
  `environments`, or a UI module (surface, react context) whose `environments`
  names `workerd` or `node`; a module body or `dx.config.ts` importing
  `./operations` or `../operations/<file>` instead of `#operations`; a
  `#operations` imports entry with `node` but no `workerd` key in a plugin whose
  `./<X>Plugin` export has a `workerd` condition; a workerd-registered operation
  handler importing `@dxos/react-ui*`; a hand-written `*.workerd.ts` plugin file
  or a separate build target added only to swap one module per runtime. Do not
  flag plugins with no `workerd` condition. Fix: list `environments` on each
  module (headless modules include `workerd`, UI modules `['browser', 'tauri']`),
  use `imports` conditions to swap implementations, and exclude a UI-bound
  handler from the workerd handler set only when it cannot be made headless.
  Source: 5 corrections, e.g. "the proper fix is adding a workerd condition to capabilities rather than reaching your hand into capabilities and grabbing the operation handler."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/*/dx.config.ts
    - packages/plugins/*/package.json
  grep: environments|workerd|operations
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule headless-leak-check-per-workerd-plugin: Every plugin with a workerd variant has a React-leak check tracing only worker conditions, with a target of zero
  Without a per-plugin check a UI import into the worker bundle goes
  unnoticed until the host fails to activate the plugin. Every composer
  plugin inherits `check-module-structure`, which traces each export
  declaring `workerd` against `.config/import-bans.yml`. Flag a plugin
  package without the `composer-plugin` tag; a local
  `check-module-structure` that replaces the inherited args; a ban copied
  into a `moon.yml` instead of the shared file; a tolerated non-zero count.
  Fix: keep the tag, put the ban in `.config/import-bans.yml`, and fix the
  violation at its source.
  Source: 4 corrections, e.g. "every plug-in should have a check for that. Why didn't we catch it?"
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/*/moon.yml
  unit: file
  context: package
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule capability-module-layout: A capability is defined inline in its contribute call, in a module named after its directory
  A separately exported const typed with the capability interface leaks an
  extra symbol and needs a manual annotation; implementation code in a
  capability directory's `index.ts` hides it; state helpers in an
  `*Capabilities` namespace mix behavior into what should be tags and
  schemas. Flag `export const x: SomeCapabilities.Foo = {...}` used only by
  `Capability.contribute`, implementation code in a capability directory's
  `index.ts`, and getters/updaters exported from a capabilities namespace. Do
  not flag implementations reused by tests or several contributions. Fix:
  inline the object in `Capability.contribute(Cap, {...})` inside
  `<dir>/<dir>.ts`, with `index.ts` re-exporting it; move state logic into
  the operation that owns the write.
  Source: 4 corrections, e.g. "should it just be defined in capability contribute, so you don't have to manually type annotate it?"
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/src/capabilities/**/*.ts
    - packages/sdk/app-toolkit/src/**/*.ts
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule create-then-add-operations: Create instantiates in memory and AddObject persists; don't add composite create-and-file operations
  A bespoke `AddCompanionChat` / `CreateXAndAdd` duplicates the sequence every
  caller can already express and blurs what `Create` means. Flag a new
  operation that bundles instantiation with `db.add`, a `Create*` operation
  that adds to the database, and new Remove/Move-style operations with no
  caller yet. Do not flag operations that hold real domain logic beyond
  sequencing. Fix: invoke the `Create` operation, then
  `SpaceOperation.AddObject` with the holder as target.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "Rather than having an add companion chat operation, I would expect us to just call create, and then call add object from the space operations."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: Operation|\.add\(
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule one-operation-per-navigation-intent: One operation call carries a navigation intent; don't chain operations that race
  Calling `SwitchWorkspace` then `Open`, switching from two places, or
  navigating empty then replacing produces races and flicker. Flag several
  layout/navigation operations invoked in sequence for one user intent, or
  two modules each deciding the same landing. Do not flag distinct user
  intents. Fix: compute the target first (expand, pick the first child),
  then make one call carrying every input (`Open({ subject, workspace })`),
  with a single owner for the decision.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "Why are we calling SwitchWorkspace twice? ... We should be trying to reduce the number of these calls to avoid these races."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/plugins/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: SwitchWorkspace|LayoutOperation
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule static-path-bindings: Declare a URL/path binding statically under its owner's key, not with a runtime query
  A path that follows from the id structure needs no `db.query` resolver,
  and borrowing another feature's binding key or minting parallel keys
  (`project-session`, `project-artifact`) breaks ownership. Flag an async
  path resolver that queries the database for a derivable mapping, an
  extension reusing another feature's key, and sibling keys where one key
  with deeper ids would do. Do not flag mappings whose shape really depends
  on data (nested collections, file-system entries). Fix: a static def under
  the owning type's key, distinguishing children by id depth.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "why can this path not be a static def?"
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/src/capabilities/**/*.ts
  grep: path:
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-startup-permission-prompts: A plugin that asks for a browser permission at startup is never enabled by default
  A permission prompt on boot (HID, USB, notifications, camera) greets every
  user of every environment with a dialog they did not ask for. Flag a
  permission API (`requestPermission`, `requestDevice`, `navigator.hid`,
  `getUserMedia`) called during plugin activation, and such a plugin (or any
  new labs plugin) added to a default plugin set. Do not flag a request made
  from a user gesture after the plugin is enabled. Fix: request on user
  action, and leave the plugin out of the defaults.
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "we should not use any permission APIs on startup. And if a plug-in does it should not be enabled by default in any environment."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/apps/composer-app/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: requestPermission|requestDevice|navigator\.(hid|usb|serial|bluetooth)|getUserMedia|defaults
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule subpath-lint-not-adhoc-entrypoints: Expose a package's modules as namespaces via the subpath lint, not hand-added entry points
  Hand-adding `./types`, `./operations`, or `./lazy` exports to split out a
  few symbols creates one-off entry points that bypass the
  import-as-namespace convention. Flag a new `exports` subpath in a
  `package.json` whose package is not enrolled in the subpath lint, or a
  subpath exporting loose functions instead of a namespace. Do not flag the
  lint-required namespace subpath of an enrolled package. Fix: enroll the
  package in the subpath lint, turn the modules into namespace files, and let
  the fixer rewrite imports.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "rather than adding types and operations entry point, why don't you just add assistant toolkit to the subpath lint?"
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/package.json
  grep: "exports"
  unit: file
  context: diff
  severity: warn
```

## UI

```mdl
rule operation-owns-ui-state-transitions: Order close/clear transitions in the operation; views don't cache stale state to hide them
  A component that keeps "the last non-null value" after its state was
  cleared (`useLastPresent`, a ref mutated during render, content kept after
  `open=false`) masks an ordering bug and keeps stale data on screen;
  reshaping persisted state to fix ordering is the same mistake. Flag either
  in a diff. Do not flag animation state local to a primitive. Fix: in the
  operation, flip the open flag first and clear content once exited, keeping
  the state shape.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "useLastPresent is also bad, all the logic should live in the operation, you should clear the things in the proper order."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/src/**/*.tsx
    - packages/plugins/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/ui/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: useLast|lastPresent|useRef
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule stable-component-for-optional-content: Render one always-present component that handles its empty case
  Choosing between two components on data that may arrive a commit later
  (`<Solo/>` vs `<Split/>`, `<Companion/>` vs `<CompanionEmptyPlank/>`)
  remounts the subtree when the data lands, and a column or slot rendered
  only once data loads shifts the layout after boot. Flag JSX that switches
  component type, or adds a layout slot, on whether content exists. Do not
  flag gating on a real open/closed flag. Fix: keep one component at a
  stable position that renders its own empty state, and reserve the slot up
  front.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "we want just one component here and for it to be rendered unconditionally, and handle the empty case within it."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

## Testing

```mdl
rule minimal-precise-tests: Add the fewest tests that pin the change, each precise and readable
  Overlapping tests for one fix, assertions on state the code cannot touch,
  and branching or polling test conditions cost review time and hide what is
  actually guarded. Flag several tests asserting the same path with trivial
  variations; assertions on untouched state; a pure function tested through
  a full database fixture; copy-pasted setup; test conditions a reader must
  trace (nested predicates, multi-step booleans); tests that pass with and
  without the fix. Do not flag one regression test per distinct bug,
  table-driven rows that are distinct cases, or property tests. Fix: one
  small test per behavior, minimal direct inputs, shared setup in one named
  helper, and a direct `expect` on the observable outcome.
  Source: 9 corrections, e.g. "minimize the number of tests you add, keep tests precise and focused and clear and readable."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.test.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.test.tsx
    - packages/**/src/playwright/**/*.ts
  unit: file
  context: diff
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule one-test-suite-per-module: A module's tests live in its one test file; an operation's tests in a suite named after it
  A sibling `X.feature.test.ts` next to `X.test.ts` splits one module's
  coverage across files, and a mixed `describe` in an operations folder hides
  which operation is under test. Flag a new test file for a module that
  already has one, and an operations test suite not named after one
  operation. Do not flag files for test kinds that need a different runner or
  tag (browser, workerd, e2e, `*.integration.test.ts`). Fix: fold the cases
  into the existing file as a nested `describe`, one suite per operation.
  Source: 5 corrections, e.g. "One suite per module."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.test.ts
  unit: file
  context: siblings
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule per-test-mutable-fixtures: Mutable test state is created per test; stateless layers stay module-level
  Module-level mutable fixtures (registries, managers, scratch records) leak
  state between tests; moving a pure Layer into `beforeEach` adds churn for
  nothing. Flag a module-level `let` or mutable object shared across tests,
  and a stateless Layer rebuilt per test. Fix: create stateful fixtures in a
  closure or `beforeEach` inside the `describe`; keep stateless layers as
  module constants.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "any state that we're testing against should be created within a closure."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.test.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.test.tsx
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-swallowed-assertions-in-e2e-helpers: E2E helpers never catch an assertion to retry or ignore it
  `.catch(() => {})` on an `expect`, or a click-and-retry fallback in
  `.catch(async () => ...)`, turns a failing check into a pass and buries an
  app defect. Flag such catches in Playwright specs, page objects, and test
  harnesses, and `void p.catch(() => {})` silencing the loser of a hand-rolled
  race. Do not flag a catch that adds diagnostics and rethrows, or deliberate
  `expect.toPass` / `expect.poll`. Fix: `Locator.or()` for "either",
  web-first assertions or `toPass` for "eventually", and waiting for the
  expected app state before acting.
  Source: 4 corrections, e.g. "what's the point of expect if its caught?"
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/playwright/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/testing/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/*.spec.ts
  grep: \.catch\(
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-retries-or-skips-for-flaky-tests: Fix a flaky test's cause; don't add retries, CI skips, or lean on quarantine
  `isolate-benchmark-setup-and-flaky-tests` forbids downgrading a flaky test
  to reporting-only; this covers the other ways to hide it. Flag added
  `retry:` / `retries:` on a test, describe, or Playwright project;
  `skipIf(CI)` / `test.skip` on a flaky test; a test-side workaround for a
  product bug; treating Trunk quarantine as the pass signal for a change.
  Do not flag a skip linked to an issue blocked on an external upstream fix.
  Fix: reproduce with retries off, gather evidence until the mechanism is
  proven, then fix the product or the test's real wait condition.
  Source: 4 corrections, e.g. "the goal is to TRACK DOWN and FIX ALL THE ISSUES not to hide them."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.test.ts
    - packages/**/*.spec.ts
    - packages/**/playwright.config.ts
    - packages/**/vitest.config.ts
  grep: retr(y|ies)\s*:|skipIf|\.skip\(
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule dont-bend-product-for-tests: Don't change product behavior so a test passes; make the test drive the real flow
  Removing a deliberate UI rule (locked branches, disabled toggles) because a
  test trips on it, or adding app-level guards against a race only test
  automation can hit (Playwright clicking faster than a person), degrades
  the product to suit the test. Flag such product changes whose stated
  motivation is a test. Do not flag a real regression the test correctly
  caught, or a race real users can reach. Fix: update the test to wait for
  the state a user would see, and match the behavior from before any
  regressing rewrite.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "The only reason this is happening is because playwright can click faster than a user."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: pr
  context: diff, pr
  system-one: off
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule test-at-owning-package: Test behavior in the lowest package that implements it, not through a plugin
  A plugin test re-verifying logic from a core or SDK package is slower, sits
  far from the code, and duplicates guarantees the framework already tests.
  Flag a new test under `packages/plugins/**` that exercises logic
  implemented in a lower-level package, or re-verifies a framework guarantee.
  Do not flag tests of wiring that exists only in the plugin. Fix: move the
  test next to the implementation; drop the redundant one.
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "the test should probably go at a lower level than the plugin."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/src/**/*.test.ts
  unit: file
  context: imports
  severity: warn
```

## Code style

```mdl
rule named-variants-not-undefined: Name every case of a decision result; don't let undefined or a boolean stand for one
  `'reject' | 'link' | undefined` (where `undefined` means "move") or
  `boolean | 'link'` forces readers to know an unnamed case, and a second
  callback beside an existing one answering half the same question splits
  one decision in two. Flag a result union using `undefined`/`false` as a
  meaningful case, and a new callback prop parallel to an existing one. Do
  not flag `undefined` meaning "absent" on an optional input, or a cheap yes/no
  prefilter. Fix: one callback returning an explicit named union
  (`'move' | 'link' | 'reject'`), exported as a type.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "Would it be better to have a named value rather than undefined as the 3rd case?"
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: \|\s*undefined|boolean\s*\|\s*'
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule flat-dispatch: Dispatch messages and events flat, not through nested conditionals
  An `onmessage` handler or reducer three levels deep in `if`/`typeof`
  branches is hard to read and easy to break. Flag dispatch on message kind
  and state nested three or more levels, accumulator-plus-search logic, and
  `() => () => {}` placeholders. Do not flag one- or two-branch handlers.
  Fix: a discriminated union with a flat `switch` or handler map, early
  returns for guards, and small named collaborators.
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "make the logic on the worker less of a nested conditional mess."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: onmessage|addEventListener\(['"]message|switch \(
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule constants-not-nullary-factories: Export a fixed value as a constant, not a zero-argument function
  `companionRelation()` that always returns the same value reads like it
  computes something. Flag an exported function taking no meaningful
  arguments that returns the same value each call. Do not flag factories that
  allocate fresh mutable state. Fix: export a constant (`AppNode.companion`)
  and a separate helper for the rare variant.
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "AppNode.companionRelation() -> AppNode.companion?"
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: export const \w+ = \(\) =>
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-coined-jargon: Name things with plain domain terms, not metaphors coined while implementing
  Identifiers and log lines built on invented jargon ("house session",
  "ticket trailer") and fields nothing explains or reads (`aspect`) cannot be
  decoded by the next reader. Flag such a name or an unexplained, unread new
  field. Do not flag established project or protocol vocabulary. Fix: rename
  to the literal role (`internalSessionId`, `footer`), or remove the field.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "what is a house session?"
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: diff
  system-one: off
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule named-durations-and-budgets: Write durations as unit arithmetic and time budgets as shared named strategies
  `300000` hides "five minutes", and a local `SLICE_MS = 8` per loop scatters
  tuning that should be named once. Flag raw millisecond literals of five or
  more digits, local time-slice constants, and a hand-rolled
  `performance.now()` yield loop where the shared yield helper exists. Do not
  flag small self-evident values or `Duration` helpers. Fix:
  `5 * 60 * 1_000` or `Duration.minutes(5)`, and the shared
  `yieldOrContinue('interactive' | 'smooth' | 'idle')`.
  Source: 4 corrections, e.g. "having interactive, smooth and idle yield strategies ... Then we don't need to hard code constants everywhere."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: \b\d{5,}\b|_MS\s*=\s*\d|performance\.now\(\)
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule code-not-in-strings: Keep shipped or executed code in a real file, not a template string
  A probe script or C source inside a template literal gets no editor
  support, lint, or type checks. Flag a multi-line string literal holding a
  program that is later run, shipped, or compiled. Do not flag one-line shell
  commands or short SQL. Fix: move it to its own file and import it with
  `?raw` or read it from disk.
  Source: 2 corrections, e.g. "I don't like having code just inside of a string ... have it as an actual code file that you import with the ?raw syntax."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - tools/**/*.ts
  grep: `[^`]*(#include|\bfunction\b|\bimport\b|\bconst\b)
  unit: file
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule todo-has-owner: Every new TODO names its owner
  An unattributed TODO has nobody to ask and nobody to finish it; the repo
  convention is `TODO(handle)`. Flag an added `TODO` or `TODO:` without a
  person handle, or with a topic tag (`TODO(depot-ci)`) instead. Do not flag
  untouched pre-existing TODOs. Fix: `TODO(<github-handle>): ...` using the
  PR author unless the user names another owner.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "All to do should be attributed."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/*.tsx
    - tools/**/*.ts
    - .github/**/*.yml
  grep: TODO(?!\([A-Za-z0-9_-]+\))
  unit: file
  context: diff
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-lint-suppression-to-pass: Don't add a lint-disable comment to get past a rule; fix what it points at
  A suppression silences the rule for the next change too. Flag an added
  `eslint-disable`, `oxlint-disable`, `@ts-ignore`, or `@ts-expect-error`.
  Do not flag a suppression citing an upstream bug the rule cannot express.
  Fix: restructure or rename to satisfy the rule (e.g. module naming for
  import-as-namespace), or change the rule config with justification.
  Source: 1 correction, e.g. "why does this need to disable lint?"
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: (eslint|oxlint)-disable|@ts-(ignore|expect-error)
  unit: file
  context: diff
  severity: warn
```

## Process

```mdl
rule one-changeset-per-pr: A PR carries at most one changeset, and none for a chore
  Several changesets per PR fragment the release notes, and a changeset on a
  tooling or config fix announces a change no consumer sees. Flag more than
  one new `.changeset/*.md` in a PR, or a changeset on a PR with no
  consumer-visible change. Do not flag changesets inherited from main. Fix:
  merge them into one that summarizes the whole PR, or delete it for chores.
  Source: 3 corrections, e.g. "one changeset only please."
  scope: repo
  files:
    - .changeset/*.md
  unit: pr
  context: pr
  severity: warn
```
